  => STEP 5 PASS on all four actions. `replace` substitutes the relay's own field for the
     client's, `keep` forwards the client's untouched, `append` preserves the client's
     and adds the relay's own after it (two distinct TLVs in one frame, confirmed from
     the raw bytes), and `drop` discards the request entirely -- the control that must
     not match, and it produced exactly zero.

STEP 6 -- option 82 is stripped from the reply delivered to the client (RFC 3046)  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Measured on the client side in EVERY leg above. It is a real measurement here because
  the scapy server ran with --echo-option82 throughout, so each OFFER arriving at the
  relay from the server CARRIED option 82. In every leg the OFFER that reached the client
  on eth3 carried none:
   02:e8:00:00:20:01 -> OFFER 192.168.10.100  option82: ABSENT (stripped)
   02:e8:00:00:20:02 -> OFFER 192.168.10.101  option82: ABSENT (stripped)
   02:e8:00:00:20:03 -> OFFER 192.168.10.102  option82: ABSENT (stripped)
   ... identically for every client of every insertion leg (baseline-off,
       agent-option-on, remote-id-ascii, checking-on-with-opt82, checking-on-no-opt82,
       policy-keep, policy-append, post-reboot)

  => STEP 6 PASS: the relay removes the agent information before handing the reply down
     to the client, as RFC 3046 requires, in every configuration tested -- including the
     legs where the field it inserted was a configured ASCII string and the legs where
     the client's own field had been kept or appended.

